---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="SQL Analytics & Data Engineering"
  eyebrow="POSTGRESQL CASE STUDIES"
  description="Two PostgreSQL case studies showing both sides of analytical work: extracting business insight from relational data and preparing external data so it can be trusted downstream."
  heroImage="/images/projects/sql-retail-performance.png"
  heroAlt="Retail Performance Analytics Tableau dashboard"
  tools="PostgreSQL · SQL · Python · Tableau"
  metrics={[
    { value: '1.54M', label: 'retail transactions' },
    { value: '$98.7M', label: 'revenue analyzed' },
    { value: '97', label: 'partner transactions staged' },
    { value: '84', label: 'exact customer matches' }
  ]}
  links={[
    {
      label: 'Open Tableau',
      href: 'https://public.tableau.com/app/profile/ryan.wenstrom2339/viz/SQLAnalyticsDataEngineering/RetailPerformanceAnalytics'
    },
    {
      label: 'View GitHub',
      href: 'https://github.com/Ryan-Wenstrom-Portfolio/sql_analytics'
    }
  ]}
>
  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Two analytical problems</h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="detail-card">
        <h3>Retail performance</h3>
        <p>
          Use a relational PostgreSQL dataset to analyze store performance,
          customer activity, product demand, and time-based trends across
          1.54M transactions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="detail-card">
        <h3>Partner-data pipeline</h3>
        <p>
          Convert nested external JSON into relational staging tables, validate
          references and financial fields, reconcile customer identities, and
          produce business-ready reporting outputs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Retail analysis</h2>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>
        The retail case study analyzed $98.7M in revenue across five stores.
        SQL was used for multi-table aggregation, customer coverage, product
        concentration, store comparisons, monthly trends, and weekday
        performance before the outputs were published in Tableau.
      </p>

      <div class="finding-grid">
        <div class="finding">
          <strong>$98.7M</strong>
          <span>Total revenue analyzed across the retail dataset.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="finding">
          <strong>1.54M</strong>
          <span>Transactions included in the relational analysis.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="finding">
          <strong>58.3%</strong>
          <span>Share of unit volume represented by the three highest-volume products.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="finding">
          <strong>5 stores</strong>
          <span>Store-level performance compared in the reporting layer.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Partner-data pipeline</h2>
    <div class="pipeline">
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>01</span><strong>Nested JSON input</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>02</span><strong>Python transformation</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>03</span><strong>PostgreSQL staging</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>04</span><strong>Reference + financial validation</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>05</span><strong>Exact customer reconciliation</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>06</span><strong>Aggregated reporting output</strong></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Pipeline evidence</h2>
    <div class="stack">
      <figure class="supporting-visual">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/sql-partner-pipeline.png"
          alt="Partner data pipeline Tableau dashboard"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Partner pipeline reporting summarizing ingestion, matching,
          validation, and financial outputs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="finding-grid">
        <div class="finding">
          <strong>86.6%</strong>
          <span>84 of 97 partner transactions matched an existing customer exactly.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="finding">
          <strong>13</strong>
          <span>Transactions routed for review instead of being force-matched.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        The partner workflow is batch-oriented rather than streaming. Customer
        reconciliation uses exact matching, so unresolved records are routed
        for review instead of being probabilistically assigned. Public
        reporting uses aggregated outputs rather than private customer data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
